      "hints": [
        "Calculate the discriminant of the quadratic $125x^2 - 128x + 192 = 0$.",
        "Show that $\\Delta < 0$, which proves that $f'(x) = 0$ has no real roots and the curve has no turning points.",
        "Check the sign of $f'(x)$ at $x = 1$ ($f'(1) = -18 < 0$) and conclude $f$ is strictly decreasing for all $x > 0$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Calculate the discriminant of the equation $125x^2 - 128x + 192 = 0$ corresponding to $f'(x) = 0$:\n$$\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac = (-128)^2 - 4(125)(192) = 16384 - 96000 = -79616 < 0$$",
        "Deduce that there are no stationary points on the curve:\n$$\\text{Since } \\Delta < 0, \\text{ the equation } f'(x) = 0 \\text{ has no real solutions, so } f'(x) \\text{ never crosses zero for } x > 0.$$",
        "Evaluate the sign of $f'(x)$ at a sample domain point (e.g. $x = 1$):\n$$f'(1) = -18 < 0 \\implies f'(x) < 0 \\text{ for all } x > 0$$\n$$\\text{Therefore, } f \\text{ is a strictly decreasing function.}$$"
      ],
